Boost.Context keeps the abi of the build request. Its default_abi rule, a <conditional> of the library's
requirements, which win over the request, chose ms whenever b2 ran on Windows, before looking at the target:
an Android build there got <abi>ms with <binary-format>elf, found no assembly for it, and libboost_context.a
lacked jump_fcontext, make_fcontext and ontop_fcontext. Where the request names no abi, the rule is unchanged.

diff --git a/libs/context/boost-context-features.jam b/libs/context/boost-context-features.jam
index 01daf2d..20755b8 100644
--- a/libs/context/boost-context-features.jam
+++ b/libs/context/boost-context-features.jam
@@ -53,6 +53,9 @@ feature.set-default binary-format : elf ;
 
 rule default_abi ( properties * )
 {
+    # an abi of the build request stays: on a Windows host the rule chose ms even for the ELF of Android
+    if [ property.select <abi> : $(properties) ] { return ; }
+
     local tmp = sysv ;
     local arch = [ property.select <architecture> : $(properties) ] ;
 
